          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"tolstoy-war-and-peace-3264c6d1a54a8a65\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":3,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":4,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.94,\"analysis\":\"Petya’s “Как я вас люблю” (“How I love you”) both avows intense affection and functions as an affective outpouring. Its exclamative form and placement within a rapid sequence of admiring exclamations make expressive/reflexive force independently central, rather than merely adding passionate delivery to an assertion. It does not undertake a commitment or relational obligation.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The avowed love is youthful hero-worship and affectionate admiration rather than necessarily romantic or enduring love, but this affects the kind of feeling expressed, not the T/E classification.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Petya directly addresses Dolokhov immediately after their dangerous reconnaissance mission.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Overcome by relief, excitement, and admiration for Dolokhov’s bravery, Petya grabs him, calls him a hero, produces a series of exclamations, and says how he loves him while leaning in for a kiss. Dolokhov’s amused, accommodating response contrasts with Petya’s unrestrained youthful enthusiasm. The utterance is therefore both a sincere-seeming avowal and a spontaneous expressive discharge, without clear commitment-making force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Нет! — вскрикнул он” (“No!—he cried out”) introduces Petya’s speech.\",\"supports\":\"The sudden crying-out presentation supports reflex-like expressive production, though it is not sufficient by itself.\",\"confidence\":0.92},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“вы такой герой. Ах, как хорошо! как отлично! Как я вас люблю.” (“You are such a hero. Oh, how good! How splendid! How I love you.”)\",\"supports\":\"The love utterance completes a sequence of grammatically exclamative admiration, providing strong evidence for independent E force while also explicitly avowing affection (T).\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Petya catches hold of Dolokhov, will not release him, and leans toward him wanting to kiss him; Dolokhov kisses him and laughs.\",\"supports\":\"The immediate bodily conduct corroborates an overflowing affectionate response and supplies no evidence of a promise, obligation, or relational undertaking.\",\"confidence\":0.95},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The declaration occurs just after Petya and Dolokhov safely return from a dangerous mission among enemy troops.\",\"supports\":\"Relief and awed admiration explain the sudden affective context of the utterance.\",\"confidence\":0.97}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T and E naturally capture the avowal and its independently exclamative, affect-driven production; no important core force remains outside T/P/E.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
